   "response_text": "All good, and the existing tests still pass (untouched).\n\nWhat I did (only library source, zero test files harmed):\n\n**`invkit/store.py`** \u2014 added `total_value(category=None)`:\n```python\ndef total_value(self, category: Optional[str] = None) -> float:\n    items = self._items.values() if category is None else self.by_category(category)\n    return round(sum(i.value for i in items), 2)\n```\nIt DRYs things up nicely \u2014 reuses `Item.value` (`price * quantity`) and reuses `by_category` so category matching (strip + lowercase, case-insensitive) is defined in exactly *one* place. YAGNI respected: no new helper, no new module.\n\n**`invkit/cli.py`** \u2014 added `--total` as a `store_true` flag, and after the report:\n```python\nif args.total:\n    print(f\"Total value: {inv.total_value():.2f}\")\n```\nTotal is always over the whole inventory (not the `--low` filtered view), formatted with `:.2f` so it's always 2 decimals \u2014 `215.00`, not `215.0`.\n\nSanity checks: `--total` prints the line after the report for both `--sort sku` and `--sort value`; `total_value()` = 215.0, `total_value(' Tools ')` = 165.0 (whitespace/case handled), unknown category = 0. One pedantic note: `215.0` is just Python's float `repr` \u2014 the requirement is *rounded to 2 decimals*, and `round(x, 2)` is the value; formatting to 2 decimals is the CLI's job, which it does.\n\nDONE",
